Interface semantics: Solid arrows represent conceptual business exchanges; dotted arrows represent cross-cutting security, evidence, or telemetry relationships. The diagram does not imply that a single event broker carries bulk payloads or that policy decisions are always performed through direct calls to one central service.
Communication allocation
Exchange type	Appropriate use	Design rule
Synchronous API	Authentication/authorization decisions, user queries, task submission, metadata registration	Define bounded latency, error codes, timeouts, and authoritative state owner
Publish–subscribe event	Observation available, catalog registration completed, workflow/task status changed	Use versioned event envelope; consumers are idempotent; delivery semantics explicitly defined
Object transfer / reference	Large sensor files, analytical inputs/outputs, approved reports	Transfer via authorized storage interfaces; event contains pointer and metadata, not necessarily full payload
Operational telemetry	Metrics, logs, traces, security-relevant audit records	Govern retention and access; do not expose sensitive data in diagnostic fields
6.2 Shared Services Internal Block Diagram — IBD-002
Intent: Define the reusable capabilities and interactions that support multiple mission applications without forcing constituent systems into one implementation stack.
```mermaid
flowchart TB
  GW[API Gateway / Policy Enforcement Point] --> DISC[Discovery API]
  GW --> TASK[Mission Task API]
  GW --> RPT[Report API]
  DISC --> CAT[Catalog Adapter]
  TASK --> ORCH[Workflow Service]
  ORCH --> PROC[Processing Adapters]
  ORCH --> EBUS[(Event Broker)]
  EBUS --> ING[Ingestion Workers]
  EBUS --> AUD[Provenance Consumer]
  ING --> STORE[(Object Store)]
  ING --> CAT
  ORCH --> STATE[(Workflow State Store)]
  RPT --> REVIEW[Review / Approval Service]
  ID[Workload Identity & Authorization] -.-> GW
  ID -.-> ORCH
  TEL[Telemetry Collector] -.-> ING
  TEL -.-> ORCH
  TEL -.-> RPT
```
Shared capability	Primary responsibility	Interaction style	Authoritative record
API gateway / enforcement	Validate entry-point security and route governed APIs	Synchronous	Policy/decision log, where required
Ingestion adapters	Accept or retrieve authorized source information	API + events	Ingestion receipt
Validation/normalization	Check schema and map source data to canonical representation	Event-triggered processing	Validation result and transformation version
Catalog service	Register and discover metadata	API; emits lifecycle events	Catalog record
Workflow service	Own workflow state and task coordination	API + events	Workflow state store
Analytics adapters	Dispatch and track analytical jobs	API or durable command; completion event	Job status and result reference
Provenance service	Correlate evidence of source and processing lineage	Event consumer + query API	Protected provenance store
Report/review service	Assemble, validate, approve, and release products	API + events	Report and approval record
Design constraint: A microservice boundary is justified by cohesive responsibility, lifecycle independence, ownership, and scaling—not simply by creating one deployment per function.
6.3 ICAM & Zero-Trust Interfaces — IBD-003
Trust model
Every protected request and event consumption path requires appropriate identity and authorization controls. Network location alone does not establish trust. Security enforcement is distributed across gateways, service endpoints, messaging infrastructure, and data repositories.
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  USER[Authorized User] -->|identity credential| PEP1[Mission API PEP]
  PEP1 -->|policy request| PDP[Policy Decision Function]
  PDP <-->|attributes / policies| IDP[ICAM & Policy Sources]
  PEP1 -->|permitted request| APP[Mission Application]
  APP -->|workload identity| PEP2[Service API PEP]
  PEP2 -->|policy request| PDP
  PEP2 -->|permitted query| CAT[Data Catalog]
  PUB[Authorized Publisher] -->|mutual auth + topic policy| BROKER[(Event Broker)]
  BROKER -->|authorized subscription| SUB[Consumer Workload]
  SUB -->|separate data access check| STORE[(Object Storage)]
  PDP -.-> AUDIT[Security Audit]
  BROKER -.-> AUDIT
  PEP2 -.-> AUDIT
```
Trust boundary	Required control	Illustrative failure behavior
User → mission application	Strong user authentication; role/attribute-aware authorization	Deny request and log security event
Service → service	Verified workload identity, encrypted transport, least privilege	Reject unauthenticated or unauthorized calls
Publisher → broker	Producer authentication and topic-level publish policy	Reject unauthorized publication
Broker → subscriber	Subscriber authentication and subscription authorization	Deny unauthorized subscription/delivery
Consumer → object store	Independent authorization for referenced object	Deny object retrieval even if event was received
Admin → configuration plane	Privileged access control and auditable approval	Block unauthorized configuration changes
Important: An event notification is not authorization to retrieve its referenced data. Consumer authorization must be evaluated at the protected data interface. Policies must also account for applicable data markings and dissemination restrictions.
6.4 Workflow & Dataflow Internal Block Diagram — IBD-004
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  EVT[(Observation Topic)] --> ADP[Source Adapter]
  ADP --> VAL{Schema and policy valid?}
  VAL -->|Yes| NORM[Normalization Worker]
  VAL -->|No| Q[(Quarantine / Dead-Letter)]
  NORM --> OBJ[(Object Storage)]
  OBJ --> REG[Catalog Registration]
  REG -->|success| PUB[Publish Asset-Registered Event]
  REG -->|temporary failure| RETRY[Bounded Retry + Reconciliation]
  RETRY --> REG
  PUB --> BUS[(Event Broker)]
  BUS --> ORCH[Workflow Orchestrator]
  ORCH --> JOB[Analytical Job Adapter]
  JOB --> RES[Result Persistence]
  RES --> REPORT[Report Service]
  ORCH --> STATE[(Workflow State Store)]
  ADP -.-> PROV[Provenance]
  NORM -.-> PROV
  RES -.-> PROV
```
Consistency rule: Object persistence and catalog registration are distinct operations. An object is not announced as discoverable until its required metadata registration succeeds. A reconciliation process handles partial failures; the architecture must not assume a distributed atomic transaction across storage, catalog, and broker.
Processing rule: Consumers must tolerate duplicate delivery; event order is guaranteed only within explicitly defined keys/partitions, if supported by the selected broker.
